  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            Most homes in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a>, <a href="/areas/st-cloud" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">St. Cloud</a>, and <a href="/areas/central-florida" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Central Florida</a> have textured walls and ceilings—usually orange peel or knockdown. When you <a href="/services/drywall" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">repair drywall</a>, matching the existing texture is critical so the patch blends in. Here's how we match drywall texture in Florida homes based on 15+ years of patch work.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Common Drywall Textures in Central Florida Homes</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. Orange Peel Texture</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Most common in Florida.</strong> Orange peel looks like the skin of an orange—small, rounded bumps all over the surface. Applied with a texture sprayer (hopper gun) that sprays thinned joint compound onto walls or ceilings. Dries quickly, easy to paint over. Popular in newer homes (1990s+) and most Kissimmee/Orlando subdivisions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Knockdown Texture</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Knockdown starts as orange peel but is lightly flattened with a trowel while still wet. Creates a flatter, more random pattern with peaks and valleys. Common in high-end homes, spec homes, and custom builds. Slightly more expensive than orange peel but still affordable. Popular in Celebration and upscale Orlando neighborhoods.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Smooth (No Texture)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Some newer Florida homes (especially modern builds or high-end custom homes) have smooth, untextured drywall. Smooth walls show imperfections more easily and require precise mudding and sanding. Less common than textured walls in Kissimmee/Orlando.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">4. Popcorn Texture (Ceilings Only)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Popcorn (or acoustic) texture was popular in 1970s-1990s Florida homes. Bumpy, cottage-cheese-like ceiling texture. Many homeowners remove it because it's dated and hard to clean. If your home has popcorn ceilings, see our <a href="/guides/popcorn-ceiling-removal-kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">popcorn ceiling removal guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">How to Match Drywall Texture</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Matching texture requires the right tools, technique, and practice. Here's the process:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 1: Repair the Drywall Hole or Crack</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Patch the hole with drywall, tape the seams, apply joint compound (mud), and sand smooth. The patch should be flush with the surrounding wall before texturing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 2: Apply Texture to the Patch</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>For orange peel texture:</strong> Load thinned joint compound into a texture sprayer (hopper gun). Spray a light, even coat over the patched area. Let it dry. The spray creates the rounded bumps that match orange peel.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>For knockdown texture:</strong> Spray orange peel texture as above. While still wet (5-10 minutes after spraying), lightly flatten the peaks with a knockdown knife (wide trowel). This creates the knockdown pattern. Timing is critical—too wet and you smear it, too dry and it won't flatten.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>For smooth walls:</strong> No texture needed. Sand the patch perfectly smooth, prime, and paint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 3: Blend the Edges</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Texture the patch slightly beyond the repaired area so it blends into the existing texture. Feather the edges so there's no hard line where new texture meets old. This is the hardest part—matching texture density, pattern, and blend requires experience.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Step 4: Prime and Paint</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Once texture is dry (24 hours), prime the patched area and paint to match the existing wall color. Texture shows through paint, so even a perfect texture match won't blend if the paint color is off.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Our take:</strong> Texture matching is harder than it looks. Orange peel is easier to match than knockdown because it's consistent. Knockdown requires the right timing and trowel technique to flatten the peaks correctly. Bad texture matching leaves a visible patch—the repair stands out instead of blending in. We practice on scrap drywall to match the pattern before texturing the actual wall.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Why Texture Matching Fails</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Wrong texture type:</strong> Spraying orange peel on a knockdown wall (or vice versa) never matches.</li>
            <li><strong>Too thick or too thin:</strong> Texture density must match the existing wall. Too thick looks heavy, too thin looks flat.</li>
            <li><strong>Poor blending:</strong> Hard edges where new texture meets old show the patch.</li>
            <li><strong>Timing issues (knockdown):</strong> Flattening too early or too late creates the wrong pattern.</li>
            <li><strong>Mismatched paint:</strong> Even perfect texture won't blend if paint color or sheen is different.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">DIY vs. Professional Texture Matching</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>DIY texture matching:</strong> Possible for small patches if you have a texture sprayer and practice. Orange peel is easier to DIY than knockdown. Expect a learning curve—first attempts rarely match perfectly.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-4 font-sans">
            <strong>Professional texture matching:</strong> We match texture daily and can blend patches so you can't see the repair. Cost: $75-$300 depending on patch size. Worth it if the patch is visible (living room, bedroom) or if DIY attempts failed.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Florida-Specific Texture Considerations</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <li><strong>Humidity slows drying:</strong> Texture takes longer to dry in humid months. Plan extra time between texturing and painting.</li>
            <li><strong>Mold-resistant compound:</strong> Use mold-resistant joint compound for bathroom and kitchen repairs in Florida.</li>
            <li><strong>Smooth walls are rare:</strong> Most Central Florida homes have textured walls, so texture matching skills are essential for drywall repairs here.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Common Questions</h2>
          
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">Can you match any drywall texture?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                Yes. Orange peel and knockdown are most common in Central Florida, and we match them regularly. Custom textures (skip trowel, swirl) are harder but still matchable with the right technique.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">How long does texture take to dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                24 hours in normal conditions, longer in humid Florida summers. You can prime and paint once texture is fully dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">Can I paint over texture without priming?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                No. New joint compound (mud and texture) absorbs paint unevenly, leaving a dull, blotchy finish. Always prime first, then paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-white border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-2 tracking-wide">What if my texture doesn't match?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
                Bad texture can be scraped off (while wet) or sanded down (once dry) and re-done. If you're not confident in your texture matching skills, call a pro before painting—fixing a bad texture match is easier before paint goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most Central Florida homes have orange peel or knockdown drywall texture. Matching texture requires the right tools (texture sprayer), technique (spray density, knockdown timing), and practice. Orange peel is easier to match than knockdown. Professional texture matching costs $75-$300 per patch and ensures seamless blending. DIY is possible for small patches if you have a texture sprayer and patience. See our <a href="/guides/drywall-repair-cost-kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">drywall repair cost guide</a> for pricing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Need Drywall Texture Matched?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
